  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3}>\\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3}.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the factor $2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n0\\le {sin}^{2}\u2061x\\le 1\\,\u21d2\\,-3\\le 2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3\\le -1.\n$$\n\nSince $2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3$ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3} & >\\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3} \\\\ (2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3)\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3} & <(2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3)\\cdot \\frac{4-3x}{2{sin}^{2}\u2061x-3} \\\\ x+2 & <4-3x \\\\ 4x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
